Now I have full understanding of the bug. Let me write the plan.

**Root cause:** In `django/db/models/base.py`, the `parent_links` dict (lines 195-207) is built by iterating over all `OneToOneField` instances. When multiple `OneToOneField`s point to the same parent model (e.g., both `document_ptr` with `parent_link=True` and `origin` without), the last one processed overwrites the earlier one in the dict. Since iteration is by field definition order, a non-parent-link field defined after a parent-link field incorrectly becomes the stored parent link. This causes `options.py` line 254-256 to raise `ImproperlyConfigured` because the field it promotes to PK lacks `parent_link=True`.

**Fix:** In the loop at lines 204-207, when a key already exists in `parent_links`, only overwrite if the incoming field has `parent_link=True`. This ensures explicit parent links always win over other OneToOneFields pointing to the same model.

**This is a single-file fix.** The downstream consumer at `options.py:245` (`next(iter(self.parents.values()))`) correctly follows whatever field is stored in `self.parents`, so once `base.py` stores the right field, `options.py` works correctly. All other consumers of `self.parents` (deletion.py, base.py `_set_pk_val`) use the stored field for its actual purpose (finding the parent link pointer) and will work correctly once the correct field is stored.

```json
{
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "django/db/models/base.py",
      "why": "Contains the __init_subclass__ logic that builds the `parent_links` dict (lines 194-207). When multiple OneToOneFields point to the same parent model, the last one processed overwrites the earlier one. If a non-parent-link field is defined after a parent-link field, the wrong field is stored and later causes ImproperlyConfigured in options.py."
    }
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "target": "django/db/models/base.py : parent_links population loop (lines 204-207)",
      "change": "Modify the inner loop body to only overwrite an existing parent_links entry for a given key if the incoming field has `parent_link=True`. Change from unconditionally assigning `parent_links[make_model_tuple(related)] = field` to: compute the key, then set it only if the key is absent or the new field has `parent_link=True`.",
      "expected": "When multiple OneToOneFields target the same model, the one with `parent_link=True` is always stored in `parent_links`, regardless of field definition order. This means `parent_links[{Document_key}]` correctly stores `document_ptr` (with parent_link=True) instead of `origin` (without parent_link=True)."
    }
  ],
  "completeness_check": "I checked all call sites that consume `self.parents` or `parent_links`: options.py (lines 241-257 for PK setup, line 626 for get_ancestor_link), base.py (line 572 for _set_pk_val), deletion.py (lines 190, 254 for cascade logic). All of these just use whatever field is stored in `self.parents`; none independently re-derive which field has parent_link. The only place that decides which field goes into `self.parents` is the `parent_links` dict in base.py. No system checks or validators touch parent_link. So fixing the single loop in base.py is complete."
}
```

The investigation confirms this is a single-file, single-line fix. The issue is on line 1076 of `django/db/models/sql/query.py` in the `resolve_lookup_value` method, where the resolved list of values is always cast to a `tuple`, regardless of whether the original input was a `list` or a `tuple`. The fix preserves the original container type.

```json
{
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "django/db/models/sql/query.py",
      "why": "Line 1076 unconditionally casts the resolved values to a tuple, ignoring whether the input was a list. This breaks field types that depend on matching input types (e.g. PickledField). This is the only place that needs changing — it's the sole definition of resolve_lookup_value and the only site where the list→tuple coercion happens."
    }
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "target": "django/db/models/sql/query.py : resolve_lookup_value (line 1076)",
      "change": "Replace `value = tuple(resolved_values)` with `value = type(value)(resolved_values)`.",
      "expected": "When the input `value` is a `list`, the method returns a `list`; when it is a `tuple`, it returns a `tuple`. The container type is preserved, so exact-value queries on ORM fields that compare input types (e.g. PickledField) work correctly again."
    }
  ],
  "completeness_check": "I confirmed that `resolve_lookup_value` is only defined once (line 1053) and called from exactly one call site (line 1258). I searched for any other tuple-cast pattern in the same package (`django/db/models/sql`) and found no other occurrences. Only this single line needs to change — no downstream consumers depend on the value always being a tuple."
}
```

Now I have a clear understanding of the bug. Let me summarize the root cause:

**Root Cause:** In `formfield_for_manytomany()` (line 242 of `options.py`), the admin unconditionally overwrites the `widget` kwarg with autocomplete/raw_id/filter widgets — there is no `if 'widget' not in kwargs:` guard. In contrast, `formfield_for_foreignkey()` (line 218) correctly checks `if 'widget' not in kwargs:` before assigning a default widget. This means any widget passed by the user via `kwargs` (e.g. when overriding `formfield_for_manytomany()` in a custom ModelAdmin) is silently ignored.

```json
{
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "django/contrib/admin/options.py",
      "why": "This file contains both `formfield_for_foreignkey` (which works correctly) and `formfield_for_manytomany` (which has a missing guard). Only `formfield_for_manytomany` needs to be fixed here."
    }
  ],
  "steps": [
    {
      "target": "django/contrib/admin/options.py : formfield_for_manytomany (line 252)",
      "change": "Wrap the widget-assignment block (lines 253-261) with `if 'widget' not in kwargs:`, exactly as `formfield_for_foreignkey` does at line 224. The block that sets `kwargs['widget']` for autocomplete, raw_id, and filter_vertical/filter_horizontal should only run when no widget was explicitly provided by the caller.",
      "expected": "When a caller passes `widget=...` in kwargs to `formfield_for_manytomany()`, that custom widget is preserved and not overwritten by the admin's default widget logic. The behavior becomes consistent with `formfield_for_foreignkey()`."
    }
  ],
  "completeness_check": "Only `formfield_for_manytomany` in `django/contrib/admin/options.py` needs to change. The subclass override in `django/contrib/auth/admin.py` (GroupAdmin.formfield_for_manytomany) merely calls `super().formfield_for_manytomany(...)` and passes kwargs through, so it naturally benefits from the fix. No other callers or producers of the widget kwarg for this method exist in the source tree."
}
```

